  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through early next week. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will cross the forecast area this morning, and \nwill be mostly moisture starved but there is some light showers.\nA prefrontal trof is exiting the Downeast coast this morning\nwith rain showers. Isolated to scattered rain showers along \nthe cold front this morning, however, rainfall amounts are \nlikely to remain below a tenth of an inch, with no improvement \nto drought conditions. A second round of showers will follow, \nmostly in the north this afternoon as a 500mb trof pivots\nthrough the state. Small but non-zero CAPE of up to 150J/kg \nalong with NW flow 300 to 320 degrees could result in some \nconvective showers behind the front through the afternoon and \ninto Friday evening. Unfortunately, another moisture starved \nsystem, resulting in minimal precipitation. This evening cold\nair advection will rapidly increase and 540 thickness line\nplummets to the Downeast coast along with the 1340 line. Any \nrain showers leftover will likely switch to snow showers and\ncannot rule out a dusting on cold grass surfaces.\n\nHigh pressure returns through the weekend, keeping the pattern\ndry. Models are slowing a low pressure system to our west, now\nprojecting primary and secondary FROPA to cross our CWA sometime\nlate week. PWATs are 0.50-1.25\", along with relatively dry RHs \nat the surface the precipitation would need to overcome. \nOverall, again, no improvement to drought conditions are \nexpected with these showers. \n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal. \nOverall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index blend has the \nNorthern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to Severe Dryness \ncategory, with portions of the Upper St. John Basin now dipping\ninto the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 29 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake and Millinocket are at \nRecord Low (deep) conditions on record (33-35 years POR). \nKenduskeag, Hadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much \nBelow Normal conditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal \nconditions approaching slipping down into Below Normal \nconditions. Therefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the \nentire CWA in at least Moderate Drought (D1) with now 64% of \nthe CWA in Severe Drought (D2), this has the potential to worsen\nthrough the weekend with continued precipitation deficits.",
